# THE SELF-HOST STACK BEHIND A TLS TERMINATOR IT DOES NOT OWN.
#
# Mounted at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile INSTEAD of the sibling Caddyfile when OHMAIL_EXTERNAL_TLS is
# set in .env — docker-compose.yml composes the mount's source path from that one variable, so
# the choice of door is a boolean and not a Caddy configuration surface.
#
# WHAT THIS EXISTS FOR. Three real deployments could not run this stack at all:
#
#  · a Tailscale user with a publicly trusted certificate for their `ts.net` name, from
#    `tailscale cert` or `tailscale serve`;
#  · anyone who already runs a reverse proxy in front of everything on the box;
#  · anyone behind a corporate load balancer that terminates TLS for them.
#
# The sibling Caddyfile cannot serve any of them, and not by oversight: its site address IS
# OHMAIL_ORIGIN, so telling it to serve plain HTTP would have meant telling the application a
# different origin from the one people type. Splitting the LISTEN address from the ANNOUNCED
# origin is the whole of this file. OHMAIL_ORIGIN keeps naming the https address the terminator
# presents — the cookie host, the passkey identity and every link the app writes come from it —
# and this door listens for plain HTTP on port 80 behind the thing that terminates it.
#
# THIS DOOR HAS NO CERTIFICATE AND ASKS FOR NONE. `http://:80` is an explicit HTTP site address,
# so Caddy provisions nothing, orders nothing from any public authority, and does not install the
# HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect it adds for an https site — a redirect here would bounce the terminator
# straight back at itself. Port 443 has nothing behind it on this door; with OHMAIL_BIND at its
# loopback default that stray publish is not reachable from anywhere, which is the configuration
# a same-box terminator wants anyway.
#
# ── X-Forwarded-* IS HONOURED FROM ONE ADDRESS, AND THE DEFAULT IS LOOPBACK ─────────────────
#
# `X-Forwarded-For` and `X-Forwarded-Proto` are headers any client can write. Behind a
# terminator they are the only way to learn who the caller was and over what — and in front of
# one, believing them means believing whatever a caller typed. Caddy decides that with
# `trusted_proxies`, and it is the only thing in this stack that decides it at all: no
# application code here reads `X-Forwarded-Proto` for anything, and the session cookies carry
# `Secure` as a literal on every mint rather than deriving it from a header. The boot check
# asserts that, so this door cannot become the reason a cookie stops being Secure.
#
# The default is `127.0.0.1` — a terminator on this same box, which is the common case and the
# safe one. Set OHMAIL_TLS_TERMINATOR when yours is elsewhere. Getting it wrong in the strict
# direction costs the caller's address in the audit log; getting it wrong in the loose direction
# is believing a stranger's header, so the default is the strict one.
{
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static {$OHMAIL_TLS_TERMINATOR:127.0.0.1}
	}
}

http://:80 {
	# The same route table the TLS-owning door serves — see routes.caddy.
	import /etc/caddy/routes.caddy
}
